# How do I grow sorghum in my garden or on my farm?

## Short Answer

Sorghum (Sorghum bicolor) is a versatile, heat-loving cereal grain that thrives in warm climates and tolerates drought better than almost any other grain crop.
It can be grown for grain, syrup, forage, or as a [[cover-crop]] in rotation plans.
Sorghum requires full sun, well-drained soil, and soil temperatures of at least 60°F (15°C) before planting.

## Detailed Explanation

### What is Sorghum?

Sorghum is an ancient cereal grain domesticated in Africa over 5,000 years ago.
The species [[sorghum-bicolor]] is the most widely cultivated form, grown on every inhabited continent.
It is the fifth most important cereal crop globally after wheat, rice, maize, and barley.
Sorghum is naturally gluten-free, making it increasingly popular for specialty diets.
The plant grows 3 to 8 feet tall depending on variety, with a seed head that forms a loose or compact panicle at the top.

### Climate and Site Requirements

Sorghum is a warm-season crop that performs best in USDA zones 4 through 10 when grown as an annual.
It requires 90 to 120 frost-free days to reach maturity depending on the variety.
The crop is remarkably [[drought-gardening|drought-tolerant]] once established, surviving on as little as 15 inches of annual rainfall.
Sorghum prefers full sun and well-drained soil with a pH between 5.5 and 7.5.
It tolerates poor soils better than corn, making it ideal for marginal land and [[dryland-permaculture-strategies|dryland farming]].

### Soil Preparation and Planting

Prepare the seedbed by loosening soil to a depth of 6 inches and incorporating compost or a [[cover-crops-and-green-manures|green manure]] from a previous crop.
Wait until soil temperatures reach at least 60°F (15°C) at a 2-inch depth before planting.
Sow seeds 1 to 1.5 inches deep, spacing rows 18 to 36 inches apart depending on the variety and intended use.
Thin seedlings to 4 to 6 inches apart within rows once they reach 3 to 4 inches tall.
Sorghum can also be broadcast-seeded for cover cropping at a rate of 20 to 30 pounds per acre.

### Varieties and Their Uses

Grain sorghum varieties produce compact seed heads and grow 3 to 5 feet tall.
Sweet sorghum varieties have juicy stems used for making syrup and grow 8 to 12 feet tall.
Forage sorghum and [[sorghum-almum]] are used for livestock feed and can reach heights of 10 feet or more.
Broom corn varieties are grown for their long, fibrous seed stalks used in broom-making.
The [[permaculture-sorghum-guide|permaculture approach to sorghum]] emphasizes multi-purpose varieties that provide grain, mulch, and biomass.

### Watering and Fertilizing

Sorghum is remarkably water-efficient due to its C4 photosynthetic pathway.
Once established, it typically needs only 1 inch of water per week during the growing season.
Excessive nitrogen can cause lodging (falling over), so apply moderate amounts or rely on [[cover-crop-species-comparison|legume cover crops]] in rotation for nitrogen.
A balanced fertilizer applied at planting is usually sufficient for home garden production.
Side-dress with a light nitrogen application only if plants appear pale or stunted during early growth.

### Pest and Disease Management

Sorghum is relatively pest-resistant but can be affected by aphids, fall armyworms, and sorghum midge.
Bird damage to ripening seed heads is one of the most common challenges for small-scale growers.
Using [[companion-planting|companion planting]] with sunflowers or other tall plants can help distract birds.
Grain mold can develop in wet conditions during seed head maturation.
Crop rotation following [[crop-rotation-principles|crop rotation principles]] helps break pest and disease cycles.
Avoid planting sorghum after other grass-family crops to reduce risk of soilborne pathogens.

### Harvesting and Processing

Grain sorghum is ready to harvest when the seeds are hard and difficult to dent with a thumbnail.
Cut seed heads from the stalk and dry them in a well-ventilated area for 1 to 2 weeks.
Thresh by rubbing the dried heads between gloved hands or using a mechanical thresher.
Winnow the chaff by pouring grain between containers in a gentle breeze.
Sweet sorghum for syrup should be harvested when the seeds are in the soft-dough stage.
The pressed juice is boiled down into a dark, flavorful syrup similar to molasses.

### What About Growing Sorghum as a Cover Crop?

Sorghum-sudangrass hybrids are one of the most popular [[cover-cropping-systems|summer cover crops]] for building soil organic matter.
They produce massive biomass, sometimes exceeding 8 tons per acre in a single season.
The deep root system breaks up compacted subsoil and scavenges nutrients from deep layers.
Mow or crimp the crop before seed formation to prevent volunteer plants the following season.
Sorghum-sudangrass produces allelopathic compounds that suppress weeds, making it an excellent [[cover-crop-multifunction-design|multifunctional cover crop]].

### What About Sorghum in Permaculture Systems?

In permaculture designs, sorghum fits well as a summer grain in polyculture plantings.
It can be intercropped with [[amaranthus-caudatus|amaranth]], cowpeas, and other warm-season crops.
The tall stalks provide wind protection and shade for lower-growing companions.
After harvest, the stalks make excellent [[cover-crop-termination|chop-and-drop mulch]] for building soil.
Sorghum also serves as a [[sorghum-seed-spawn-production|mushroom spawn substrate]] when used as a grain for inoculation.

### What About Growing Sorghum in Containers?

Dwarf grain sorghum varieties can be grown in large containers of 5 gallons or more.
Use a well-draining potting mix amended with compost and a slow-release fertilizer.
Containers dry out faster than garden beds, so monitor moisture closely during hot weather.
Place containers in the sunniest spot available, as sorghum needs at least 6 to 8 hours of direct sun.
Container-grown sorghum produces smaller yields but works well for seed saving or ornamental use.
This approach is useful for gardeners in cold climates who want to experiment with small grain production.

## Key Points

- Sorghum ([[sorghum-bicolor]]) is the fifth most important global cereal and naturally gluten-free.
- It thrives in hot, dry conditions where corn and other grains struggle, needing only 15 inches of rain.
- Plant after soil reaches 60°F, in rows 18 to 36 inches apart, and thin to 4 to 6 inches.
- Choose grain, sweet, forage, or broom varieties based on your intended use.
- Use [[sorghum-cultivation-and-uses|sorghum cultivation guides]] for variety-specific planting rates and spacing.
- Sorghum-sudangrass hybrids are elite summer [[cover-crops|cover crops]] producing massive biomass.
- The crop fits naturally into [[crop-rotation-and-soil-health-planning|crop rotation plans]] after legumes or broadleaves.
- Bird protection and proper harvest timing are the biggest challenges for small-scale grain production.

